Z||000003||2birds||05-24-2000||08:34 PM||||Thefiero64,<BR>If you're looking for a trailer hitch for your fiero, they are readily available at any place that sells them (Pep Boys, Wally World, etc).  For some reason, they don't make one that fits GT's, and I would assume SE/2M6's since the rear pan is the same.  I'm guessing body clearance is the problem, not the mounting point, though||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||05-24-2000||10:39 PM||||Makes sense,with the hubs having the bearings already,just yank the axles,weld onto the subframe,then bolt the subframe back in.<BR>A nose for the hulk could be laid-up from fiberglass over a form made from thin wood strips attached to a piece of plywood.<BR>Car camping?||||reg||1
Z||000005||wkayl||05-25-2000||06:08 PM||||I knew I had seen a picture of one.  I found it. <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierokid/dells_photos/pcenhalf.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierokid/dells_photos/pcenhalf.jpg</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||05-26-2000||12:32 PM||||It's time consuming more than hard to do a project like that. (Either that one in the picture isn't on the hitch or it needs some draw bar bending to make it sit better. Otherwise it looked pretty nice)<P>Unless you happen to be an experienced welder and trailer builder, you MUST have the draw bar built up by someone that knows what they are doing. There are more things to consider than the bar placement. If you cobb this up you could kill people when the draw bar fails and the thing goes flipping down the highway. Insurance may not cover such a failure.<P>You can do the rest anyway you want but don't take chances with the drawbar work.<P>you'll likely want to do some spring work on it. Otherwise it will likely sit at the top of the struts and stay there. To use the one in the picture, if you where doing a GT rear I'd put in base coupe springs and then chop them down to get the height right. GT springs are stiff. Base springs will give trailer a softer suspension so it doesn't bounce off the road at every pebble. If you had a rear sway on the chop car I'd take it off and put it on the tow car. The trailer will be pretty stiff as it is.<P>You can also likely drop the tire pressure a couple PSI.||||reg||1
Z||000007||TheFiero64||05-26-2000||12:40 PM||||Thanks for the advice. I have just got back online <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">. My cable modem died on me and the cable guy just left. 30 hours without the net is horrible  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000008||Monkeyman||05-27-2000||01:07 PM||||ogre--Looks like the trailer is on it's dolly.  The trailer needs a wing and the little cooler/trunk on the front needs to be painted to match the trailer/car.<P>Here's another one.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierotrailer_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
